          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#relation_to_conserved_quantities Relation to conserved quantities
          %p
            The values of the momentum map for each given Lie algebra generator may be regarded as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conserved+current"} conserved currents
            given by a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                Hamiltonian
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Noether+theorem"} Noether theorem
          %p
            Specifically if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            equipped with a “time evolution”
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+action"} Hamiltonian action
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝔓𝔬𝔦𝔰𝔰𝔬𝔫
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \to \mathfrak{Poisson}(X,\omega)
            given by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            and if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝔓𝔬𝔦𝔰𝔰𝔬𝔫
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g} \to \mathfrak{Poisson}(X,\omega)
            is some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+action"} Hamiltonian action
            with momentum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ξ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi(\xi)
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ξ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \xi \in \mathfrak{g}
            which preserves the Hamiltonian in that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            vanishes
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msup
                    %mi Φ
                    %mi ξ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\{\Phi^\xi, H\} = 0
          %p then of course also the time evolution of the momentum vanishes
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mi d
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mi t
                  %msup
                    %mi Φ
                    %mi ξ
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi H
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi Φ
                    %mi ξ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{d}{d t} \Phi^\xi = \{H, \Phi^\xi\} = 0
                  \\,.
          %p
            See at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "Noether%27s+theorem#HamiltonianNoetherTheorem"} Noether theorem – In terms of momentum maps/Hamiltonian Noether theorem
          %h3#RelationToConstrainedMechanics Relation to constrained mechanics
          %p
            In the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constrained+mechanics"} constrained mechanics
            the components of the momentum map (as the Lie algebra argument varies) are called
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+class+constraints"} first class constraints
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+reduction"} symplectic reduction
            for more.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %p
            The momentum map is a crucial ingredient in the construction of Marsden–Weinstein
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+quotients"} symplectic quotients
            and in other variants of symplectic reduction.
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The concept is originally due to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Marie+Souriau"} Jean-Marie Souriau
          %h3#general General
          %ul
            %li
